{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": " Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a significant figure in the Crusader states during the 12th Century. Born in around 1135, he succeeded his father, Raymond II, as the Count of Tripoli in 1152. His reign was marked by military conflicts, diplomacy, and the consolidation of Tripoli as a major Crusader state.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*: **Military Campaigns and Alliances**\n\nRaymond III's rule was characterized by several military campaigns aimed at expanding and securing the Crusader states. He played a crucial role in the wars against the Zengid Dynasty, particularly the campaigns against Nur ad-Din and his successors. In 1157, he participated in the Battle of Jacob's Ford, where the Crusaders achieved a significant victory. This battle was a turning point in the Crusader's efforts to maintain control over the region and to defend against advancing Muslim forces. Additionally, Raymond formed strategic alliances with other Crusader states and European powers to strengthen the Crusader's position.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*: **Relations with the Church and Religious Affairs**\n\nRaymond III maintained close relations with the Church and was a strong supporter of the Latin Patriarchate of Jerusalem. He worked to ensure the Church's influence in Tripoli and the surrounding regions. His reign also saw the construction and renovation of several religious buildings, including the Cathedral of St. Mary in Tripoli, which served as a symbol of the Crusader's presence and their commitment to the region. These efforts were part of a broader strategy to solidify the Crusader states and to integrate them more closely with the Church.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*: **Internal Administration and Governance**\n\nRaymond III implemented various administrative reforms to improve the governance of Tripoli. He introduced measures to strengthen the judiciary and to enhance the efficiency of local government. His reign saw the implementation of policies to promote economic development and to ensure the stability of the region. These efforts included the establishment of trade agreements with neighboring states and the promotion of local industries. Raymond's governance style was marked by a focus on law and order, which helped to maintain the stability of Tripoli during a turbulent period.\n\nRaymond III's reign came to an end in 1187, when he was captured by the forces of Saladin during the Battle of Hattin. He was later released, but his release did not mark the end of his influence. He continued to play a role in the political affairs of the Crusader states until his death in 1197. Raymond III's legacy as a Crusader leader is remembered for his military prowess, his diplomatic skills, and his efforts to strengthen the Crusader states during a critical period in their history.\n\nThis summary provides a detailed overview of Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, focusing on his military campaigns, religious affairs, and internal governance. It highlights the key aspects of his reign and the impact he had on the Crusader states during the 12th Century.", "logprobs": [{"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -3.199403762817383}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0015370947076007724}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.10726633667945862}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.05117441341280937}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.1709168979432434e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.915183232398704e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -2.372236667724792e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.5420295000076294}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.7982171773910522}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09327995777130127}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -1.2167367935180664}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.021660080179572105}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.12664839625358582}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.06304144859313965}, {"token": " Crus", "logpr